  "subject": "🍽️ Axios Finish Line: Dinner party decline",
  "date": "Thu, 01 Oct 2026 21:29:34 -0400",
  "body_text": "96 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n 🐆 Plus: Cubs du jour | Thursday, October 01, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n PRESENTED BY COX ENTERPRISES \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Axios Finish Line \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n By Mike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei &middot; Oct 01, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n Welcome back! Smart Brevity™ count: 331 words … 1 min. Copy edited by Amy Stern. \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 1 big thing: Why we stopped hosting \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Illustration: Allie Carl/Axios\n\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Americans are spending more time alone, eating more meals alone and socializing less. All that's exemplified in a stunning trend:\n\n The share of U.S. adults who say they host friends or family at home at least once a month has plummeted 70% since 1975, Substacker Derek Thompson writes this week. Why it matters: Having people over was once a fixture of American social life. Now, hosting is becoming a disappearing art.\n\n 📉 By the numbers: 50 years ago, 42% of Americans hosted friends or family at least monthly, per DDB Needham Life Style survey data.\n\n Today, just 12% do, Thompson writes, citing new Data for Progress polling that replicated the DDB Needham Life Style survey. Zoom in: Thompson points to several forces that have made hosting harder. Among them:\n\n 🎶 We're spending our free time differently. Americans are traveling, attending concerts and exercising far more than 50 years ago — but those activities aren't necessarily social. 🧒 Family life got busier. Today's parents spend more time caring for their kids than past generations did. Millennial dads, for example, spend four times as much time on child care as boomer dads did, Thompson notes. 📺 \"It's too much fun to be home alone,\" Thompson writes. We have a buffet of entertainment options at our fingertips, from TV shows to movies to podcasts and more. 👓 What we're watching: Younger people are pushing back on the new normal and craving more IRL socializing — from ditching dating apps to throwing parties just to eat apples together .\n\n Dinner parties are getting some love, too. Gen Z is putting its own spin on hosting, with themes, dress codes and elaborate tablescapes, Bon Appétit reports . Go deeper: How to make the most of your dinner party \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA MESSAGE FROM COX ENTERPRISES\r\n\n \r\n Meet the nonprofits making a difference \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Cox Conserves Heroes recognizes the volunteers and nonprofits helping the planet. \n\n\r\n From more than 800 submissions, three nonprofits were selected as finalists for their work advancing sustainability. \r\n Vote by Oct. 4 for your favorite and help them win up to $30,000.\n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 2. 📸 Pic to go! \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Photo: Petr David Josek/AP 4 of 5 recently born cheetah quintuplets sit by their mother, Daenerys, in their enclosure at a zoo in Prague.\n\n More on the cubs . \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n 🏁 Please invite your friends to join Finish Line . \n\n \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
